# credentials.example — the definition of the .credentials document format.
#
# This file holds no secret and is safe to commit. It documents the shape that
# mcp-ollama-web-rust reads. The live file is .credentials, which .gitignore
# excludes; copy this template there before putting a real key in it:
#
#     cp credentials.example .credentials
#     OLLAMA_CREDENTIALS_FILE=<absolute path to this checkout>/.credentials
#
# The server reads exactly one entry from the refs section below — OLLAMA_API_KEY.
# Every other entry is left unread: not loaded into the environment, not logged,
# and never quoted in an error message. A document shared with other tools is
# therefore safe to point here.
#
# Both YAML styles are accepted. This is the block form:
#
version: 1
refs:
  OLLAMA_API_KEY: replace-with-your-key
#
# The flow form reads the same way, on one line or spread over several:
#
#     refs: {OLLAMA_API_KEY: replace-with-your-key}
#
# A bare file is also accepted when you name it explicitly: its whole trimmed
# text is the key, the shape Docker secrets and systemd credentials use. That
# form is only read from a path a client names — a discovered file must look
# like this document, so an unrelated file is never sent as a token.
#
# Meta information, in one place:
#   version: 1        conventional marker; any top-level version/refs/records
#                     key marks the file as a credentials document
#   refs:             block mapping or flow mapping of reference names to
#                     single-line values
#   OLLAMA_API_KEY    the one reference this server looks up
#
# Create a key at https://ollama.com/settings/keys. A local daemon
# (OLLAMA_HOST=http://127.0.0.1:11434) needs no key at all, and no document.
